      <section id="forge-rules" data-search-group="">
  <p class="eyebrow">01 / The Forge Rules</p>
  <h2>Prohibited Trading Practices</h2>
  <details class="faq-rule" data-search-item="">
    <summary><span class="faq-label">Minimum Trade Duration</span><span aria-hidden="true">+</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="prose">
      All trades must be held for a minimum of <strong>40 seconds</strong>. This rule applies to all types of trade closures, including Stop Loss, Take Profit, manual closure, partial closure of the position, or any other method of closing the position. Rapid "scalping" or "high-frequency" entries intended to exploit execution speeds are strictly prohibited and will be flagged during the audit.
      <div class="rules-links"><strong>Check the links below for the complete descriptions of all rules:</strong>
      </div>
      <a href="https://forgeoftraders.com/faq/what-trading-practices-are-prohibited">What Trading Practices Are Prohibited?</a>
      <a href="https://forgeoftraders.com/faq/when-can-a-breach-occur">When Can a Breach Occur?</a>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details class="faq-rule" data-search-item="">
    <summary><span class="faq-label">High-Risk Layering Limit</span><span aria-hidden="true">+</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="prose">
      While layering is permitted, you are limited to a maximum of <strong>3 positions (layers) per instrument</strong>. Exceeding this limit is a violation of our risk protocols.
      <div class="rules-links"><strong>Check the links below for the complete descriptions of all rules:</strong>
      </div>
      <a href="https://forgeoftraders.com/faq/what-trading-practices-are-prohibited">What Trading Practices Are Prohibited?</a>
      <a href="https://forgeoftraders.com/faq/when-can-a-breach-occur">When Can a Breach Occur?</a>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details class="faq-rule" data-search-item="">
    <summary><span class="faq-label">Maximum Aggregated Risk Rule</span><span aria-hidden="true">+</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>All accounts are subject to a <strong>Maximum Aggregated Risk Rule</strong> based on the account's current balance. This rule applies to the combined risk of all positions open at the same time and is <strong>not calculated separately</strong> for each individual trade or position.</p>

      <p>This rule applies to both <strong>floating losses</strong> and <strong>closed losses</strong>. Any floating loss generated by currently open positions, as well as any closed loss from positions that were part of the same simultaneous exposure, will contribute to the applicable aggregated risk threshold.</p>

      <p>For <strong>Evaluation Accounts</strong>, the maximum aggregated risk limit is <strong>2%</strong> of the current account balance. For <strong>Funded Accounts</strong>, including Instant Simulated Accounts and Passed Funded Accounts, the maximum aggregated risk limit is <strong>1%</strong> of the current account balance.</p>

      <p>If a trader has multiple positions open simultaneously, all open positions will contribute to the same aggregated risk threshold. This means that the combined floating and/or closed loss exposure of all positions open at the same time must remain within the applicable account limit.</p>

      <p>A breach occurs if the aggregated risk across all simultaneously open positions exceeds the applicable percentage of the current account balance at any point. This applies regardless of whether the limit is exceeded by one position or by the combined exposure of multiple positions.</p>

      <p><strong>Enforcement clarification:</strong> For <strong>Evaluation Accounts</strong>, exceeding the Maximum Aggregated Risk Rule is considered a hard breach. However, this breach will be reviewed during the Compliance Verification process once the account reaches the verification stage before funding. If the Risk Team confirms that the rule was breached during the Evaluation stage, the account will not proceed to funding.</p>

      <p>For <strong>Funded Accounts</strong>, including Instant Simulated Accounts and Passed Funded Accounts, exceeding the Maximum Aggregated Risk Rule is considered an <strong>automatic hard breach</strong>. The account will be automatically deactivated, in the same way as breaches related to Daily Drawdown, Maximum Drawdown, or Minimum Trade Duration.</p>

      <div class="examples-box">
        <div class="examples-label">Examples</div>

        <div class="example">
          <div class="example-title">Example 1 — Single Position on an Evaluation Account</div>
          <p>If an Evaluation Account has a current balance of $50,000, the maximum aggregated risk limit is 2%, which equals <strong>$1,000</strong>.</p>
          <p>If the trader opens one position and the floating or closed loss on that position reaches more than $1,000, the Maximum Aggregated Risk Rule is breached.</p>
        </div>



        <details class="more-examples">
          <summary>Show more examples</summary>
          <div class="examples-more">

            <div class="example">
              <div class="example-title">Example 2 — Multiple Positions on an Evaluation Account</div>
              <p>If an Evaluation Account has a current balance of $100,000, the maximum aggregated risk limit is <strong>$2,000</strong>.</p>
              <p>If the trader has three positions open at the same time:</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Position 1 has a floating loss of $700</li>
                <li>Position 2 has a floating loss of $800</li>
                <li>Position 3 has a floating loss of $600</li>
              </ul>
              <p>The total combined risk is <strong>$2,100</strong>. Because the combined exposure exceeds the $2,000 limit, this is a breach of the Maximum Aggregated Risk Rule.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="example">
              <div class="example-title">Example 3 — Funded Account With Multiple Open Positions</div>
              <p>If a Funded Account has a current balance of $100,000, the maximum aggregated risk limit is 1%, which equals <strong>$1,000</strong>.</p>
              <p>If the trader has four positions open at the same time:</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Position 1 has a floating loss of $250</li>
                <li>Position 2 has a floating loss of $300</li>
                <li>Position 3 has a floating loss of $200</li>
                <li>Position 4 has a floating loss of $350</li>
              </ul>
              <p>The total combined risk is <strong>$1,100</strong>. Because the combined exposure exceeds the $1,000 limit, this is an automatic hard breach and the account will be automatically deactivated.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="example">
              <div class="example-title">Example 4 — Funded Account Within the Limit</div>
              <p>If a Funded Account has a current balance of $100,000, the maximum aggregated risk limit is <strong>$1,000</strong>.</p>
              <p>If the trader has three positions open at the same time:</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Position 1 has a floating loss of $300</li>
                <li>Position 2 has a floating loss of $250</li>
                <li>Position 3 has a floating loss of $400</li>
              </ul>
              <p>The total combined risk is <strong>$950</strong>. Because the total exposure remains below the $1,000 limit, the account does not breach the Maximum Aggregated Risk Rule at that moment.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="example">
              <div class="example-title">Example 5 — Current Balance Changes After Closed Trades</div>
              <p>If a Funded Account starts with $100,000 but the current balance decreases to <strong>$98,000</strong> after closed trades, the 1% aggregated risk limit is calculated from the current balance, not the original starting balance.</p>
              <p>1% of $98,000 equals <strong>$980</strong>. This means that the trader's combined floating and/or closed loss exposure across all positions open at the same time must not exceed $980.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="example">
              <div class="example-title">Example 6 — Current Balance Increases After Profitable Trades</div>
              <p>If an Evaluation Account starts with $100,000 but the current balance increases to <strong>$105,000</strong> after profitable closed trades, the 2% aggregated risk limit is calculated from the current balance.</p>
              <p>2% of $105,000 equals <strong>$2,100</strong>. This means that the combined floating and/or closed loss exposure across all positions open at the same time must remain within $2,100.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="example">
              <div class="example-title">Example 7 — Splitting Risk Across Positions Does Not Avoid the Rule</div>
              <p>If a Funded Account has a current balance of $100,000, the maximum aggregated risk limit is <strong>$1,000</strong>.</p>
              <p>The trader opens five smaller positions at the same time, each risking $250. Although each individual position is below $1,000, the total combined exposure is <strong>$1,250</strong>.</p>
              <p>Because the rule is based on aggregated exposure, not individual position risk, this is an automatic hard breach.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="example">
              <div class="example-title">Example 8 — Positions Opened at Different Times but Active Together</div>
              <p>If a trader opens Position 1 at 10:00 and then opens Position 2 at 10:15 while Position 1 is still open, both positions are active at the same time.</p>
              <p>If both positions create combined exposure above the applicable risk limit, the Maximum Aggregated Risk Rule is breached. The positions do not need to be opened at the exact same second — what matters is whether they were open <strong>simultaneously</strong> and contributed to the same aggregated exposure.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="example">
              <div class="example-title">Example 9 — Evaluation Account Breach Reviewed During Verification</div>
              <p>If an Evaluation Account has a current balance of $100,000, the maximum aggregated risk limit is <strong>$2,000</strong>.</p>
              <p>If the trader exceeds this limit during the challenge, it is considered a hard breach. However, for Evaluation Accounts, this breach will be reviewed during the Compliance Verification process once the account reaches the verification stage after completing the challenge.</p>
              <p>If the Risk Team confirms that the rule was breached, the account will not proceed to funding.</p>
            </div>

